; SPDX-License-Identifier: CC-BY-SA-4.0
; caveats.vla - the model's caveats: the Caveats sheet, a thing this game gets wrong a
; row, each beside what the real world does, Management's response and the cell it
; qualifies (docs/OMAKASE.md, MENU.5 and BD-5). Words alone: one macro named after this
; file, whose body is one call of the library's strings. The source includes this file
; after its manifest, with every other include, and calls (caveats) where the sheet's
; rows belong, the one content sheet it shows among the tabs, since a player reads it
; whole. Its first row is the game's plain statement that it is not advice, which the
; certificate quotes (GUARD.1), so a row added goes at the end. To add a row, read
; README.md beside this file: a row of a field a line, its real world accurate, in the
; voice of a model's owner answering a finding, approved by a reviewer.

(defmacro (caveats)
  "the model's caveats: the Caveats sheet, a thing this game gets wrong a row"
  (strings "Caveats"                ; the sheet, which the source shows among the tabs
    ("Things this game gets wrong" 104   ; its fields, each its header and its width in characters: the simplification, plainly
     "In the real world" 115        ;   what the world does, plainly, which a finance educator reads before a release
     "Management's response" 103    ;   the joke: the model's owner answering the finding
     "Where" 13)                    ;   the cell or range it qualifies, as a formula writes one, or empty for the whole game
    ; each row: its fields in the order above, the first beside its opening parenthesis and the rest a line each
    ("You. It knows nothing of your goals, your means or your timeline."
     "Advice starts from those three, from someone licensed to give it. This game is not financial advice."
     "Any resemblance to financial advice, living or dead, is purely coincidental."
     "")
    ("Every day is a market day: 365 a year, weekends and holidays included."
     "US exchanges close on weekends and about ten holidays a year, leaving about 252 trading days."
     "Weekends are available from the rank of Partner."
     "Day!B2")
    ("Tomorrow's price is a formula of the seed and the day, and anyone can read it."
     "No one knows tomorrow's price, and anyone who says otherwise is selling something."
     "Reading ahead is not insider trading, since everyone can. Compliance finds this unsettling."
     "Market!B25")
    ("The market is four waves and a drift, and each wave keeps its period to the day."
     "Real business cycles vary in length and depth, and economists still argue whether the longest ones exist at all."
     "The Kondratiev wave is fitted to a handful of 50-year cycles. Statistics has requested a larger sample."
     "Market!A8:E11")
    ("Day to day, the market barely moves: no news, no noise, no crash."
     "A broad index can move 1% on an ordinary day, and on October 19, 1987, US stocks fell more than 20%."
     "Trading in news has been halted, pending an announcement."
     "Market!B25")
    ("Beneath the waves, the market grows 7.6% a year, every year, forever."
     "US stocks have returned about 10% a year over a century, dividends included, but unevenly: 2000 to 2009 lost money."
     "Here, past performance is indicative of future results. Management asks that this not get around."
     "Market!B2")
    ("All four stocks follow the one index: none can go bust, and owning all four spreads no risk."
     "Each company has news of its own; owning many cancels much of it, and some companies do go to zero."
     "Management is pleased to report a 0% default rate, and is investigating why."
     "Stocks!E5:E8")
    ("The riskier the stock, the faster it grows: a beta of 2 trends 15.7% a year, a beta of 0.75 trends 5.6%."
     "Riskier stocks have not reliably earned more: over long stretches, low-volatility stocks did as well or better."
     "Management has decided that risk will be rewarded, and has asked the data to comply."
     "Stocks!C5:C8")
    ("The dividend is free: a share's price does not fall on the day it pays."
     "A share's price usually falls by about the dividend on the day it goes ex, since that cash has left the company."
     "Accounting has been asked not to look into it."
     "Stocks!H8")
    ("Money comes in whole cents: every price is rounded once, to the nearest cent."
     "Ledgers keep fractions of a cent: interest accrues in them daily, and the rounding comes last."
     "Fractions of a cent are swept into an account named 'Rounding (do not open)'."
     "Stocks!E5:F8")
    ("A $1 share moves a penny at a time, and on most days not at all."
     "A real stock near $1 can swing several percent a day, and under $1 it may be quoted to a hundredth of a cent."
     "Analysts rate it a Hold, which it does."
     "Stocks!E5")
    ("Numbers have no formats: a price reads in cents, 9921 for $99.21, and a yield of 2% reads 0.02."
     "A workbook formats its numbers for people: $99.21, 2.00%, 1,000,000."
     "The decimal point has been furloughed pending a review of number formats."
     "Stocks!D5:F8")
    ))                              ; the sheet's end: a caveat added goes above this line
